    {
      "path": "law/schemas/preflight-probe.schema.json",
      "sha256": "7db00223a31060ebde21512bfdb5dd95ebbf53bdf81c41e651ed48f4c86beb8e",
      "jsonPointer": "/$defs/toolchain_probe/description",
      "location": "value",
      "originalString": "Compares the observed runtime versions with the adopter toolchain manifest (ADR-CHK-0002).",
      "reference": "ADR-CHK-0002",
      "occurrenceIndex": 0,
      "stringOffset": 76,
      "context": {
        "mode": "annotation-value",
        "observedKeywords": [
          "$defs",
          "description"
        ],
        "ancestorSegments": [
          "$defs",
          "toolchain_probe"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "path": "law/schemas/preflight-probe.schema.json",
      "sha256": "7db00223a31060ebde21512bfdb5dd95ebbf53bdf81c41e651ed48f4c86beb8e",
      "jsonPointer": "/description",
      "location": "value",
      "originalString": "One preflight probe executed as a task DAG node under the `preflight-v1` runner (ADR-CHK-0001). A probe observes one fact about the host, the checkout, or a remote and compares it with `expected`. Its `class` decides the outcome of a mismatch: an `extrinsic` probe observes something outside the candidate (an environment variable, a registry, a credential, a toolchain version, the fetched base), so its failure yields `blocked`, marks every dependent blocked-environment, and is never written to the result cache, because it says nothing about the candidate; an `intrinsic` probe observes the candidate itself (a tracked file, a clean tree, a command over the checkout), so its failure yields `fail` and is cached like any other observation. The same probe list runs locally and in the pull-request lane from the same descriptor, so the planned node sets are byte-identical for the same base and candidate.",
      "reference": "ADR-CHK-0001",
      "occurrenceIndex": 0,
      "stringOffset": 81,
      "context": {
        "mode": "annotation-value",
        "observedKeywords": [
          "description"
        ],
        "ancestorSegments": []
      }
    },
    {
      "path": "law/schemas/proof-anchor-baseline.schema.json",
      "sha256": "611430f86010f6d0a39c9cd3be2d1db9523eb1806b4c0a39b8cf6fc132a7ca1f",
      "jsonPointer": "/description",
      "location": "value",
      "originalString": "The append-only baseline that the first `evidence verify --scope chain` under ADR-EVI-0002 writes to `record/proofs/anchor-baseline.json`: one entry per physical proof line under `record/proofs/work`, with its canonical path, its one-based sequence inside that file, the SHA-256 of the line bytes, and the anchor status observed. The `cutoff` is the timestamp of that first verification; a historical declaration may name only lines whose entry was observed at the first verification, so an entry observed after the cutoff is never eligible. A later verification may append entries for lines that were not yet present and may never change or remove an entry.",
      "reference": "ADR-EVI-0002",
      "occurrenceIndex": 0,
      "stringOffset": 78,
      "context": {
        "mode": "annotation-value",
        "observedKeywords": [
          "description"
        ],
        "ancestorSegments": []
      }
    },
    {
